    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching()]{.doc-object-name\n    .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Solve the assignment problem: a minimum-weight full matching of a bipartite\n    graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching()]{.doc-object-name\n    .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Solve the assignment problem: a minimum-weight full matching of a bipartite\n    graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching()]{.doc-object-name\n  .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Solve the assignment problem: a minimum-weight full matching of a bipartite\n  graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nSolve the assignment problem: a minimum-weight full matching of a bipartite graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L83571-L83683){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching(\n    top_nodes=None,\n    weighted=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Bipartite minimum weight full matching](../assets/graphs/bipartite-minimum-weight-full-matching.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three couriers, Ana, Ben, and Cho, in a column on the left, every one linked to three deliveries d1, d2, d3 on the right. The cheapest assignment, in the accent color, is Ana–d2 (3), Ben–d1 (3), and Cho–d3 (5); the other six links are faded. The table of travel times highlights those three cells and gives the total 3 + 3 + 5 = 11, even though Ana's nearest delivery is d1 (2).\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nGiven two groups, such as workers and tasks, and a cost for each possible pairing (an\nedge between the groups, weighted by its cost), the assignment problem asks for the\ncheapest way to give every member of the smaller group a distinct partner from the\nlarger group. Missing edges are pairings that are not allowed. Classic uses include\nassigning jobs to machines, drivers to ride requests, and tracking objects between\nvideo frames.\n\nThe sides are found by 2-coloring the graph (ignoring edge direction) unless they are\ngiven with `top_nodes=`. Every node of the smaller side is matched; on a tie in size,\nboth sides are fully matched. To maximize a total score instead of minimizing a cost,\nnegate the weights.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[top_nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional list of the nodes on one side (IDs or labels); all other nodes form the\n    other side. If `None` (default), the sides are found automatically.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights as costs. If `None` (default), follows the graph's\n    `weighted` flag; with `False` every pairing costs `1`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The IDs of the matched edges, in ascending order.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not bipartite, `top_nodes=` leaves an edge within one side, or no\n    matching covers the whole smaller side.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe Hungarian method (Kuhn 1955, Munkres 1957) adds the nodes of the smaller side one\nat a time and grows the matching along a cheapest augmenting path, found with\nDijkstra-like scans over reduced costs kept non-negative by node potentials. With $r$\nrows (smaller side) and $c$ columns it runs in $O(r^2 c)$ time, the same optimum that\nNetworkX obtains from SciPy's `linear_sum_assignment`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~bipartite_maximum_matching`) \n\n:   The largest matching, when no full one exists.\n\n[](`~minimum_weight_matching`) \n\n:   The same for graphs that aren't bipartite.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThree couriers and three deliveries, with travel times as weights. Ana's nearest\ndelivery is `d1`, but giving it to Ana leaves Ben and Cho with slow ones. The quickest\nplan, 11 in all, gives `d1` to Ben:\n\n::: {#9e0f146e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"Ana\", \"d1\", 2.0), (\"Ana\", \"d2\", 3.0), (\"Ana\", \"d3\", 8.0),\n     (\"Ben\", \"d1\", 3.0), (\"Ben\", \"d2\", 7.0), (\"Ben\", \"d3\", 9.0),\n     (\"Cho\", \"d1\", 6.0), (\"Cho\", \"d2\", 4.0), (\"Cho\", \"d3\", 5.0)]\n)\ncouriers = [\"Ana\", \"Ben\", \"Cho\"]\nplan = g.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching(top_nodes=couriers)\n\nlabel = g.get_label_from_id\n[(label(g.get_edge(e).source), label(g.get_edge(e).target)) for e in plan]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[('Ana', 'd2'), ('Ben', 'd1'), ('Cho', 'd3')]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Hiring a contractor\n\n`teams()` links the 8 workers of a made-up software team to the 8 tasks of a release that\neach can do, weighted by the hours it would take them. Only seven of the tasks can be\ncovered, so there's no full matching. With `contractor=True`, Quinn joins and can take\nSecurity or DevOps. Every task is then covered, in 37 hours at the least, and one of\nthe two designers, Luis, is left free:\n\n::: {#dc9621dc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nteam = ec.teams(contractor=True)\nmatched = team.bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching()\n\nlabel = team.get_label_from_id\ncovered = {n for e in matched for n in team.get_node_ids_by_edge_id(e)}\n(\n    sum(team.get_edge(e).weight for e in matched),\n    [label(n) for n in team.get_node_ids() if n not in covered],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(37.0, ['Luis'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#e2ceec7b .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nteam.layout_bipartite(align=\"horizontal\", aspect_ratio=0.4).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight_edges=matched, label_by=\"label\", height=300\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec